WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.201 --> 00:00:02.110
(audience cheering)

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:04.150
- [Narrator] The class of 2022 is heading

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:06.230
into a great job market.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.090
- Let me add my congratulations

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:13.860
to all of you for this
extraordinary accomplishment

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.810
under challenging,
unprecedented circumstances.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:22.440
- They are the most
in-demand college graduates

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.910
to enter the job market in years.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.563
- It took a long time to get any responses

00:00:27.563 --> 00:00:29.010
and then very suddenly,

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.740
I started getting many interview requests.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.097
- This job market is
the tightest for labor

00:00:36.097 --> 00:00:37.610
that I have seen.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.880
- [Narrator] The nationwide
labor shortage means grads

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.310
are in high demand, and they know it.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:44.860
- I think happiness and
the ability to be happy

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.200
at your workspace has been
more prioritized now than ever.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.400
Wanting a job that's going
to not only be something

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.600
that pays well, and is
fulfilling professionally,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.370
but also fulfilling emotionally.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:59.060
- [Narrator] So as the class
of 2022 enters the workforce

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.160
with far more leverage than
grads from recent years,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.050
how should they approach the job market?

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.360
- I casted a wide net.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.004
I was looking for jobs everywhere.

00:01:08.004 --> 00:01:11.220
New England, the
Southeast, the West Coast.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.710
- [Narrator] Top of line
for many grads is salary.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.820
- The pay was the most
important thing for me.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:16.810
I have expenses,

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:20.290
I know I will have college
loan payments coming soon.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.900
And making sure that I
am prepared for those

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.730
is the most important thing.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.470
- [Narrator] A shortage
of entry-level workers

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.270
in some sectors have
led to starting salaries

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.120
of $100,000 or more for
inexperienced workers.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.890
- They are very aware of the tumult

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:37.200
of the last few years

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.420
and are really looking for stability

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.820
and the dollar figure on that paycheck

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.680
is going a long way for many individuals.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.640
- [Narrator] Flexibility is also important

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.380
to these recent grads.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.910
Because new graduates
can afford to be choosy,

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.150
some are taking their
time to lock in a new job.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:56.720
According to a recent survey,

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.360
about half of new grads who hadn't decided

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.890
on a job had gotten at least
one offer without accepting.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.990
- There are students that I can think of

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.850
who have had offers in hand

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.880
that they are just about to accept

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.010
and then are contacted by recruiters

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.210
from other organizations
to say we urgently

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:18.100
have an opportunity we
want you to consider.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.820
- [Narrator] That's what
happened to Austin Bowles

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.010
who recently accepted a
position as a staff electrician

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.230
at Northern Stage, a theater in Vermont.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.050
- To be totally honest here,

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.350
Northern Stage actually wasn't
the first place I accepted.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:32.700
I accepted another job,

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.490
a summer stock job is what we call it.

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.600
It's a temporary only for the summer.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.090
I had accepted that in South Carolina

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.160
and it wasn't until some time in March

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.350
when I got an email from Northern Stage

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:44.530
saying we'd like to interview.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.010
And so I actually had to give
them an unfortunate call,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.650
letting them know that I wouldn't go there

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:49.620
and I was gonna go here.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.500
So I do wish I had gone a little slower.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.750
- [Narrator] Outside of salary,

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.110
many recent grads have other requests

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:57.910
for their future employers.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:00.400
- I'm a first generation
low-income college student

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.457
and I very much, yes, salary was something

00:03:02.457 --> 00:03:03.690
that was very important
but on top of that,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:04.860
I also wanted a company

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.350
that was gonna prioritize
my work-life balance,

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.260
and I also wanted to be able to do work

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.360
that I felt very proud of

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.890
but also was very interested in.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.860
- [Narrator] Eugene Thomas
recently graduated from Yale.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.180
After interning at John
Deere over the summer,

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.440
he took a position with
the company in Iowa.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.850
- Students are very
discerning and thoughtful

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:26.760
about how the organization's
values line up

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:27.990
with their values.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:32.070
And that is certainly
around issues of diversity,

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:33.380
equity and inclusion.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.650
- And I felt really comfortable
with the entire team

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:36.620
of engineers at John Deere.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.770
I was able to visit
Waterloo, Iowa at some point

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:39.870
throughout the summer.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:40.930
Every single one of the members

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.320
of the engineering team made
me feel very comfortable.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.580
- [Narrator] One of the biggest challenges

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.640
for recent grads is housing,

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:48.320
which has been made more difficult

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.220
by the recent surge in rental prices.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.827
- The place I'm currently
working with here

00:03:51.827 --> 00:03:54.580
at Northern Stage, they
have subsidized housing

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.230
and that was a key factor in the decision.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.480
- I am from New York City

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:01.900
and I guess there was some inventive

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.060
to work in a place where cost of living

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.080
is considerably lower than New York City

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.750
and even New Haven, Connecticut.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:09.110
- [Narrator] Median apartment rents

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.810
are up more than 60% in the past year

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:15.790
and up 28% since January of 2017.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:17.950
- They saw their families impacted

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:21.030
by the Great Recession years ago.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.910
They certainly have
seen people be impacted

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:25.230
by the pandemic and
corresponding implement crisis

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:26.310
that resulted from that,

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:30.570
and the tight finances that
people felt during those times.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.930
And to some degree are still feeling.

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:34.880
So I think the rental prices

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.460
and the concern about where will I live

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:37.680
and how will I finance this

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.680
are connected to the salary conversations

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.880
that folks are having.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.800
- [Narrator] After years
of working remotely,

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.890
Grubb said many students
wanna get into the field.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.340
- I have talked personally
with a few students

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:52.147
who worked for organizations
as a summer intern,

00:04:52.147 --> 00:04:54.960
and those internships were fully virtual.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.367
And while these students are very grateful

00:04:57.367 --> 00:04:59.530
to have those experiences,

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:02.390
they feel like they missed
out on opportunities

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:04.930
to connect with other
people in their workplaces.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.974
- Hands-on experience
was very important to me,

00:05:06.974 --> 00:05:08.880
especially I think the class
of '22 is really special

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:09.850
in that respect,

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:11.730
especially in industries like engineering

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.250
because a lot of your classes
during your junior year

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.310
would have been done more
hands-on and in person

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:19.250
and that entire year was
remote, at least for Yale.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:21.490
On top of that, my summer internship,

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:22.818
with the exception of one week was remote,

00:05:22.818 --> 00:05:25.380
and I was able to have a lot
of great experience there

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:26.260
and a lot of exposure,

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:29.720
however, I would have
loved if it were in person,

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:33.520
COVID permitting to have
more hands-on experience,

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.270
and I'm really excited to get some

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:36.688
throughout my post-grad job.

00:05:36.688 --> 00:05:39.438
(dramatic music)

